from mk import mk
mk("EwxNeZttFEA","Crazy Percussions Out of a Pad in Serum 2",
 "drone-to-percussion-transient-gated-warp-mid-harmonic-eq-texture-layer-parallel-room",
 "Turn a flat sustained drone into rolling shaker-style percussion: loop and crossfade a stable bar, re-envelope it into rhythmic hits with a transient-preserving time-stretch gate at a fast note rate, then low-cut, boost one tuned mid harmonic, layer an organic shaker texture, snap it with a transient shaper and add a short parallel bright room",
 "Recycle a pad/drone into tuned percussion without a drum source. Pick the steadiest, least peaky bar of the drone, loop it with short crossfades to avoid clicks, consolidate, then use a transient-preserving warp/stretch mode with a fast transient rate (the presenter starts at a 1/60-type rate for shaker speed) and reduce the per-transient sustain/envelope so each grid step becomes a hit. Add a fade-in, consolidate again, high-pass the lows, boost the strongest mid harmonic so the hit is in key (the presenter's was G#), layer a shaker/tambourine texture (less hold, more attack, a little decay, slight pitch, denser), add a transient shaper (attack up, sustain way down), tidy with EQ and finish with a short, bright, no-pre-delay room in parallel (~22 ms, mixed under the dry).",
 ["DAW with a transient-preserving time-stretch mode and per-transient envelope (e.g. a Beats/transient mode), or Web Audio: a GainNode envelope retriggered on a 1/16–1/32 grid (implementation note)","Parametric EQ, transient shaper, texture/noise layer source, short room reverb on a parallel chain"],
 [("drone","audio","Sustained pad or drone with a steady section",True),("hit_rate","enum","Grid rate for the generated hits (fast for shaker)",True),("key_harmonic","note","Harmonic to boost so the percussion sits in key",False)],
 [("percussion_loop","audio","Rolling tuned percussion loop rendered from the drone"),("fx_chain","list","EQ → texture layer → transient shaper → EQ → parallel room")],
 ["Spoken values only: fast transient rate (~'60 notes' wording), room ~22 ms with no pre-delay and little depth. Other moves are by ear.","Consolidate after warping so the gating is printed into the waveform before further processing.","Keep the low end cut; percussion of this type lives in the mids and tops."],
 [("Find the most stable, least peaky bar of the drone and loop it. Crossfade at each loop join so there are no clicks, then consolidate to one file.","prepare","Seamless flat drone loop"),
  ("Set a transient-preserving stretch mode with linear behaviour, choose a fast transient rate for shaker speed, then reduce the transient sustain/envelope until each step becomes a short hit. Raise level, add a small fade-in, and consolidate again.","rhythmic gate","Drone becomes a rhythmic, dynamic percussive pattern"),
  ("EQ: high-pass the low frequencies that are not part of the percussion, then boost one or two mid harmonics so the part has a pitch that fits the tune or groove.","tuned eq","Hits have a clear tonal centre in the mids"),
  ("Layer an organic texture (tambourine/shaker-type) with less hold, more attack, a little more decay, slight pitch shift and higher density. EQ the texture to fill what is not poking through and reduce its decay.","texture","Sounds like real shakers rather than gated noise"),
  ("Add a transient shaper: raise attack, remove a lot of sustain, adjust speed so it is snappy but not overdone.","snap","Tighter, punchier hits"),
  ("Final EQ: bring up the gap left by processing, roll off extremes, and let the main harmonic breathe.","final eq","Balanced, perky percussion"),
  ("Put a very short bright room (no pre-delay, little depth, ~22 ms) on a parallel chain beside the dry signal and mix it under.","space","Hits gain depth without washing out")],
 ["Rendered loop shows distinct transient peaks on the grid where the drone was flat.","One mid harmonic dominates an analyser and matches the track key.","No clicks at loop joins or hit onsets.","Muting the parallel room reduces depth but keeps the rhythm intact."],
 ["percussion","resampling","transient-shaper","texture-layer","drone","parallel-reverb","hybrid-score"],
 note="DAW-specific warp UI mapped to a generic transient gate.")

mk("Py4XQbjvn4o","Philtor 12 Band Sequencer Filter",
 "sequenced-stereo-12-band-filterbank-rhythmic-pad-q-slope-lr-alternation-random-trigger",
 "Animate a static pad with a stepped 12-band stereo filterbank: assign groups of bands to sequencer patterns (offset ups vs a big down note), set a Q slope with wider low bands and narrower high bands, tame resonant peaks with mild drive plus smoothing compression, alternate bands L/R for width, and use per-band versus group random triggering",
 "Turn a sustained pad into rhythmic, moving texture with a sequenced filterbank of 12 bands per side. Use each band's volume as an EQ to keep what works. Assign subsets of bands to a step sequencer pattern (first five bands, then three frequencies with an offset 'up' pattern, then the last bands as one big down note). Increase the Q globally to get pointed peaks but apply a slope so low bands stay wider and high bands narrower. Add a little drive and a negative 'pull' (compression-style) setting to smooth peaks that blow up at high Q. Spread by sending alternate bands to left and right (sending the same band to both stays mono). Use random triggering per band ('S': each band triggers independently) or grouped ('A': grouped bands fire together) for subtle versus extreme motion. Distinct from our filter-spread and phaser skills: discrete stepped bandpass bank.",
 ["Filterbank with ≥10 bandpass bands and per-band gain (any multiband/EQ plugin with automation, or Web Audio: 12 BiquadFilterNodes per channel with gain lanes, implementation note)","Step sequencer or automation lanes per band","Compressor/saturator/limiter after the bank"],
 [("pad","audio","Static sustained synth pad",True),("band_groups","list","Which bands each sequencer pattern drives",True),("q_amount","number","Global Q with low-to-high slope",True)],
 [("animated_pad","audio","Rhythmic, stereo, filtered pad"),("band_map","table","Band → pattern group → L/R side")],
 ["No numeric frequencies or Q values are spoken; set them by ear.","High Q creates pointed peaks that can blow up: always keep smoothing compression or a limiter after the bank.","Lower bands want a smaller Q (wider) and higher bands a larger Q; use a slope rather than editing each band.","Rendering the result to audio saves CPU versus many live instances."],
 [("Insert the 12-band stereo filterbank on a static pad and use the band volumes as an EQ, turning down only the bands that are too much.","setup","Pad tone shaped, still static"),
  ("Raise the global Q from fully open (no contour) until the bands become pointed, and apply a slope so low bands are wider and high bands narrower.","q slope","Distinct resonant peaks across the spectrum without boomy lows"),
  ("Add a little drive and set the compression/'pull' control negative so peaks are smoothed without much distortion.","tame","Resonant peaks no longer jump out"),
  ("Assign the first five bands to a step pattern; then unassign them and give three frequencies an offset pattern of up notes; finally give the last bands a single big down note.","sequence","Static pad gains rhythmic movement across registers"),
  ("Alternate bands between left and right (flip-flop) for stereo spread, choosing the assignment that works in the mix.","width","Pattern moves across the stereo field"),
  ("Enable random triggering: per-band mode makes each band trigger independently (subtle, dense); grouped mode makes grouped bands fire together (sparser, more extreme). Fine-tune band volumes to contour the result.","random","Evolving, less predictable motion")],
 ["Bypassing the sequencer returns a static pad; enabling it shows rhythmic band movement on a spectrum view.","No band peak clips the output at high Q with smoothing on.","Mono-summing still keeps all bands (each band is on one side, not phase-cancelled).","Per-band random mode sounds denser than grouped mode."],
 ["filterbank","bandpass","step-sequencer","rhythmic-filter","stereo-width","pad-animation"],
 note="Device-specific labels (S/A random modes, 'pull') mapped to generic per-band vs grouped random trigger and compression.")

mk("aPsWzEfgEOo","Tessera by Audio Damage (Tuned Sequenced Resonator)",
 "scale-locked-12-voice-sequenced-resonator-bank-wave-strum-arp-swarm-noise-exciter",
 "Scale-locked resonator bank as an instrument: up to 12 tuned resonator voices on a root and scale (e.g. D minor 9, 6 voices, octave 3), excited by a stereo noise burst or drums, with motion modes (wave, strum, arpeggio, swarm), feedback/decay/damping/spread/diffusion and gated live-key voicing",
 "Use a bank of tuned resonators as a playable texture generator. Tune all voices to a root note and scale chord (example: root D, minor 9, 6 voices in octave 3) and pick a resonator model (string, bar, glass harmonica, bowl, membrane, bell, plate, tube; strings add position and brightness). Excite it with a short stereo white-noise burst (ADSR with no sustain, two steps), kicks, snares, hats, pads or piano. Animate which voices sound with a motion mode: wave (amplitude, per-voice frequency spread, phase, rate), strum (spread, curvature, direction up/down/alternate, alternate rate), arpeggio (rate, gate, up/down/up-down/random) or swarm (centre, cohesion, rate). Shape the tail with feedback, decay, damping, stereo spread and diffusion, plus vibrato, and switch to live keys or gated mode to set voices from held chords. More specific than our single harmonic-resonator and noise-crackle skills: a multi-voice scale bank with voice-motion sequencing.",
 ["Resonator bank (modal/comb resonators per voice) with scale tuning, or Web Audio: N bandpass/comb resonators tuned from a scale table with per-voice gain envelopes (implementation note)","Exciter source (noise burst generator, drum loop, synth pad, piano)","Optional step sequencer or MIDI keyboard"],
 [("root_scale","object","Root note and scale chord (e.g. D, minor 9)",True),("voices","integer","Number of active resonator voices, 1–12",True),("motion_mode","enum","wave | strum | arp | swarm | live keys | gated",True),("exciter","audio","Noise burst, drums or tonal input",True)],
 [("resonant_texture","audio","Tuned, moving resonant texture locked to the scale"),("motion_settings","table","Mode-specific parameters used")],
 ["Spoken examples only: root D, minor 9, 6 voices, octave 3, up to 12 voices, 11 resonator models. No preset names copied.","Feedback is mode-dependent: arpeggio mode has none; resonance feedback can build up fast, so trim output and use dry/wet.","Gated mode is silent unless keys are held, which lets you play chords alongside it."],
 [("Insert the resonator bank on a source. Set root (lockable) and scale, then the number of voices and octave (example D minor 9, 6 voices, octave 3).","tune","Any input rings in the chosen chord"),
  ("Choose a resonator model; test kick, snare and hats through it, trimming output when loud. On string models also set position and brightness.","model","Distinct timbres per model: string, bar, glass, bowl, membrane, bell, plate, tube"),
  ("Build a noise exciter: an ADSR-gated stereo white-noise generator with no sustain, triggered on two steps of a sequencer.","exciter","Short noise bursts become pitched resonant chords"),
  ("Pick a motion mode. Wave: adjust amplitude, per-voice frequency spread (more chaotic), phase and rate. Strum: spread, curvature, direction (one way, opposite, alternating) and alternate rate. Arp: rate, gate, up/down/up-down/random. Swarm: centre, cohesion (tight to loose) and rate. Set steps and window size, tempo-synced if wanted, and randomise.","motion","Voices enter in patterns instead of all at once"),
  ("Shape the tail: feedback, decay, damping, stereo spread, diffusion; add vibrato rate/depth; balance dry/wet.","tail","Controlled resonant sustain with stereo diffusion"),
  ("Switch to live keys so the held notes set the voices (one note, two notes, four notes), or gated mode to sound only while keys are held. Try a pad or piano as input with mix fully wet.","play","Playable resonant chord texture following the keyboard")],
 ["Every input note/hit resonates at pitches in the chosen scale only.","Changing motion mode audibly changes the order in which voices sound.","With gated mode on and no keys held, output is silent.","Raising damping shortens and darkens the tail."],
 ["resonator","modal-synthesis","scale-lock","noise-exciter","strum","swarm","arpeggio","texture"],
 note="Product demo; factory presets and bank names not reproduced.")
